   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the uniform city court act, in relation to an application for relief in aid of arbitration in a city court",
    "basePrintNo": "S4594",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4594-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld & add §206, UCCA",
    "lawSection": "Uniform City Court Act",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4594\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the uniform city court act, in relation to an application for\nrelief in aid of arbitration in a city court\n\nThis is one in a series of measures being introduced at the request of\nthe Chief Administrative Judge upon the recommendation of her Local\nCourts Advisory Committee.\n\nThis measure establishes a new section 206 for the Uniform City Court\nAct to authorize a City Court to confirm, vacate, or modify an\narbitration award, and determine the question of the arbitrability of\na controversy in an action commenced in a City Court outside New York\nCity.\n\nCurrently, the Uniform City Court Act places a significant\nrestriction on the relief that can be granted by City Courts in aid of\narbitration. Section 206 of that Act limits the City Court to entering\n\"judgment on the award of the arbitrators.\"\n\nThe effect of this limiting language has been underscored by a number\nof courts which have interpreted this language as meaning that a City\nCourt does not have the authority to confirm, vacate, or modify an\narbitration award. See Worldwide Asset Purchasing, LLC, v. Carter, 17\nMisc.3d 1132(A), 2007 WL 170845 (Plattsburgh City Court 2007); MBNA\nAmerica Bank, N.A. v. Cae, 2 Misc.3d 355 (White Plains City Court\n2003). Both courts based their respective decisions on a comparison\nof section 206 with analogous provisions in the New York City Civil\nCourt Act and the Uniform District Court Act, both of which\nexpressly confer on those respective courts the authority to confirm,\nvacate, or modify an arbitration award.\n\nThis measure, which adopts the language in section 206 of the\nUniform District Court Act, would expressly confer upon City Courts\nthe jurisdiction to grant relief in aid of arbitration set forth in\nthe CPLR, such as confirmation of the award (CPLR 7510), vacating\nor modifying an award (CPLR 7511), protecting the right to\narbitration of a pmiy to an agreement who becomes deceased or\notherwise incompetent (CPLR 7512), authorizing inclusion of the\narbitrator's fees and expenses in the arbitration award (CPLR 7513),\nand entering judgment on the award (CPLR 7514). In addition, this\nmeasure would also expressly authorize City Courts to determine the\nquestion of the arbitrability of a controversy in an action commenced\nin City Court. Finally, this measure would continue to authorize the\nrules of the City Court to provide for \"systems of arbitration and\nconciliation of claims within the jurisdiction\" distinct from the\narbitration authorized CPLR Article 75, such as an arbitration\nprogram established under Part 28 of the Rules of the Chief Judge\nwhich provides for the disposition of a civil action for money by an\narbitrator.\n\nThis measure, which would have no fiscal impact upon the State, would\ntake effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have become a law.\n\n\n2011 Legislative History:\nNone. New proposal.\n\n",
